#---
# purpose: "one lambda closed over an int and over a float are two constructions, and the forwarder routes each to the body its captures were stored for"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# fz-kdt.127. `P.run/2` forwards its callable, and the forwarder erasure drops
# the closure literal, so both calls share ONE `run` body and the choice of
# `twice` specialization is left to a runtime test. The two values differ only
# in what they captured -- `#lambda(int)` and `#lambda(float)` -- so the test
# has to name the CONSTRUCTION each value was minted from, not merely the
# lambda: a closure's heap word at `+8` is the construction wrapper's address,
# and a wrapper is one function at one capture layout.
#
# `Q` is `P` mirrored: the float call first. Arrival order must not decide the
# answer, and each module has its own forwarder so each order gets its own
# callsite.
defmodule P do
  fn twice(f, x), do: f.(f.(x))
  fn run(f, x), do: twice(f, x)
  fn mk(c), do: fn (n) -> n + c end
end

defmodule Q do
  fn twice(f, x), do: f.(f.(x))
  fn run(f, x), do: twice(f, x)
  fn mk(c), do: fn (n) -> n + c end
end

fn main() do
  dbg(P.run(P.mk(1), 10))
  dbg(P.run(P.mk(0.5), 10))
  dbg(Q.run(Q.mk(0.5), 10))
  dbg(Q.run(Q.mk(1), 10))
end
